  "grid_title": "WHAT EACH ONE DOES",
  "why": "Ibuprofen (like Advil or Motrin) and naproxen are NSAIDs, anti-inflammatories: they block the COX enzymes that make prostaglandins, the body's signals behind pain, fever and swelling, so they ease all three. Prostaglandins also protect the stomach lining, so NSAIDs can upset or irritate it (and can cause ulcers or bleeding); with heavy or long-term use, or when you're dehydrated, they're hard on the kidneys. Aspirin is an NSAID too, and it switches off platelets for the rest of their life, so blood clots less; some heart patients take a low dose, only on a doctor's advice. Don't give aspirin to children or teenagers with a viral illness like flu or chickenpox: it's linked to Reye's syndrome, which is rare but serious. Acetaminophen (paracetamol; like Tylenol) eases pain and fever but not swelling; it works mostly in the brain and spinal cord, and exactly how isn't fully understood. It's gentler on the stomach, but too much can seriously damage the liver, and it's in many cold and flu medicines, so check every label to avoid doubling up. Follow the label, and ask a pharmacist or doctor if you're unsure.",
